      "text": "To solve for \\( f(2) \\), we start with the given functional equation:\n\\[ f(x) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 4^x. \\]\n\nFirst, let's substitute \\( x = 2 \\) into the equation:\n\\[ f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 4^2 = 16. \\]\nSo, we have:\n\\[ f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 16. \\quad \\text{(1)} \\]\n\nNext, let's substitute \\( x = \\frac{1}{2} \\) into the original equation:\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) = 4^{ \\frac{1}{2} } = 2. \\]\nSo, we have:\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) = 2. \\quad \\text{(2)} \\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\( f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 16 \\)\n2. \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) = 2 \\)\n\nTo eliminate one of the variables, we can solve these equations simultaneously. First, we multiply equation (2) by 2:\n\\[ 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 4 f(2) = 4. \\quad \\text{(3)} \\]\n\nNow we add equation (1) and equation (3):\n\\[ (f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)) + (2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 4 f(2)) = 16 + 4. \\]\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[ f(2) - 4 f(2) = 20, \\]\n\\[ -3 f(2) = 20, \\]\n\\[ f(2) = -\\frac{20}{3}. \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( f(2) \\) is \\(\\boxed{-\\frac{20}{3}}\\).",
