      "text": "To solve for \\( f(2) \\), we start with the given functional equation:\n\\[ f(x) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 4^x. \\]\n\nFirst, substitute \\( x = 2 \\) into the equation:\n\\[ f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 4^2 = 16. \\]\nThis gives us the first equation:\n\\[ f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 16. \\tag{1} \\]\n\nNext, substitute \\( x = \\frac{1}{2} \\) into the original equation:\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) = 4^{\\frac{1}{2}} = 2. \\]\nThis gives us the second equation:\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) = 2. \\tag{2} \\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\( f(2) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 16, \\)\n2. \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) = 2. \\)\n\nTo eliminate \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\), we can multiply the first equation by 2:\n\\[ 2 f(2) - 4 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 32. \\tag{3} \\]\n\nNow we have:\n3. \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2) = 2, \\)\n4. \\( 2 f(2) - 4 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 32. \\)\n\nWe can add these two equations to eliminate \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\):\n\\[ (f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 2 f(2)) + (2 f(2) - 4 f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)) = 2 + 32. \\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[ -2 f(2) + f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) + 2 f(2) = 34, \\]\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 34. \\]\n\nNow substitute \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 34 \\) back into equation (2):\n\\[ 34 - 2 f(2) = 2. \\]\nSolving for \\( f(2) \\), we get:\n\\[ 34 - 2 = 2 f(2), \\]\n\\[ 32 = 2 f(2), \\]\n\\[ f(2) = 16. \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( f(2) \\) is \\(\\boxed{16}\\).",
