      "text": "This problem presents us with an interesting functional equation. To solve it, we will use the given equation repeatedly and then solve for the function \\( f(x) \\). Let's denote the given equation as:\n\n\\[ f(x) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 4^x \\tag{1} \\]\n\nFirst, substitute \\( x \\) with \\( \\frac{1}{x} \\):\n\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) - 2 f(x) = 4^{\\frac{1}{x}} \\tag{2} \\]\n\nNow, we have a system of two equations in terms of \\( f(x) \\) and \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\). To eliminate one of the variables, we can use these two equations. Multiply the first equation by 2:\n\n\\[ 2f(x) - 4 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 8^x \\tag{3} \\]\n\nNext, add equation (2) to equation (3):\n\n\\[ (2f(x) - 4 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right)) + (f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) - 2 f(x)) = 8^x + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}} \\]\n\\[ 2f(x) - 4 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) + f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) - 2 f(x) = 8^x + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}} \\]\n\\[ -3 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 8^x + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}} \\]\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = -\\frac{8^x + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}}{3} \\]\n\nNow, substitute \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\) back into equation (1):\n\n\\[ f(x) - 2 \\left( -\\frac{8^x + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}}{3} \\right) = 4^x \\]\n\\[ f(x) + \\frac{2(8^x + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}})}{3} = 4^x \\]\n\\[ f(x) = 4^x - \\frac{2(8^x + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}})}{3} \\]\n\nFinally, we need to find \\( f(2) \\):\n\n\\[ f(2) = 4^2 - \\frac{2(8^2 + 4^{\\frac{1}{2}})}{3} \\]\n\\[ f(2) = 16 - \\frac{2(64 + 2)}{3} \\]\n\\[ f(2) = 16 - \\frac{2 \\cdot 66}{3} \\]\n\\[ f(2) = 16 - \\frac{132}{3} \\]\n\\[ f(2) = 16 - 44 \\]\n\\[ f(2) = -28 \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( f(2) \\) is \\(\\boxed{-28}\\).",
